I’ll draft it. Unique, by The Other Man, LLC. Concept: custom interiors for autonomous vehicles — starting with the Tesla Cybercab, expanding to the Robovan and eventually rental fleets. The cabin has no steering wheel or pedals, so it becomes a blank canvas: LED screens as windows, G650-style lounges, disco modes, swivel seats, a fake yoke that spins free, twelve mood presets plus custom commissions. Bose powers the audio. Revenue model: three streams. One — individual commissions, high-ticket builds for private owners. Two — airport upsell partnerships with Avis, Hertz, Enterprise, where Unique meets arriving passengers at baggage claim and offers fantasy upgrades on the spot. Three — fleet contracts where rental companies offer Unique interiors as a premium tier, and Unique curates what customers ride in. Startup path: film one prototype cab, post the two captions we wrote, tag Bose and the rental brands, use that video to attract the first commission and the first fleet conversation. Capital needed: one vehicle to convert, interior materials, LED fabrication, Bose integration, and a small marketing push. Biggest risk: Tesla’s ownership and modification terms aren’t public yet, and NHTSA is still reviewing the no-steering-wheel design through October thirtieth. Mitigation: start with a titled, insurable vehicle like a used Transit or Model Y that proves the interior concept, then move to Cybercabs when fleet access opens. The moat isn’t the tech — it’s the brand. Unique becomes the name people associate with what an autonomous cabin can feel like, and that association is hard to copy.
